        "VOCAB|||Weather Idioms ☔|||*   **'Under the weather'** - Feeling slightly sick or unwell (*I'm under the weather*).\n*   **'Save for a rainy day'** - Put money aside for emergencies.\n*   **'Rain check'** - Postpone an invitation politely (*Take a rain check*).\n*   **'Steal my thunder'** - Take credit for someone else's idea.|||Elo: 'Take a rain check' é a forma mais educada de remarcar um compromisso.",
        "CONCEPT|||Connected Speech: Un-der-thuh-we-ther 🗣️|||Link prepositions and articles smoothly in idiomatic expressions.\n\n> 🗣️ **Connected Speech Phonetics:**\n> * *'Under the weather'* becomes **'un-der-thuh-we-ther'**\n> * *'Rain check'* becomes **'rain-check'**|||Elo: Diga 'un-der-thuh-we-ther' sem fazer pausas.",
        "EXAMPLE|||Politely Declining ☕|||*\"I'm feeling a bit **un-der-thuh-we-ther** today, can I take a **rain-check** on dinner?\"*\n\n> 🗣️ **Phonetics:**\n> *'Feeling a bit'* links into **'fee-lin-uh-bit'**.|||Elo: Recusou o convite com polidez e elegância.",
        "CULTURE|||Weather Metaphors in US Speech 🇺🇸|||Because small talk about weather is so dominant in the US, weather idioms are deeply embedded in daily corporate and casual conversation.|||Elo: Usar 'rain check' demonstra alto nível de naturalidade.",
